.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt RPI_FW_CPUFREQ 4 aarch64
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rpi_fw_cpufreq
.Nd Raspberry Pi 5 CPU clock control through the firmware
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following lines in the
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "options FDT"
.Cd "device cpufreq"
.Cd "device clk"
.Cd "device rpi_fw"
.Cd "device rpi_fw_clk"
.Cd "device rpi_fw_cpufreq"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The four cores of the Raspberry Pi 5 share one clock, and the VideoCore
firmware owns it.
The firmware runs the cores at the lowest rate it allows until the operating
system asks for more.
The
.Nm
driver asks: it offers the range of rates the firmware reports to
.Xr cpufreq 4 ,
so that
.Va dev.cpu.%d.freq
and
.Xr powerd 8
work.
.Pp
The levels are the firmware's maximum, every multiple of 100 MHz below it,
and the firmware's minimum.
The maximum is the one the firmware was configured with; the driver does not
exceed it.
The voltage, the power and the transition time of a level are not known to
the driver and are reported as unknown.
.Pp
A rate is set through the clock provider
.Xr rpi_fw_clk 4
and then read back from the firmware.
A rate the firmware did not take is an error.
Because the clock is shared, a rate set for one core is the rate of all.
.Pp
The firmware lowers the clock by itself when the chip is hot or its supply
is low.
The rate
.Xr cpufreq 4
reports is therefore the level nearest to the rate the firmware reports at
that moment, which need not be the level last asked for.
.Pp
Unless told otherwise, the driver leaves the clock as it finds it.
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.rpi_fw_cpufreq.attach_freq
A rate in MHz to ask for once, when the clock is first available.
It is limited to the firmware's range.
The default, 0, leaves the clock alone.
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The first instance has these read-only variables:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va dev.rpi_fw_cpufreq.0.rate
The clock's rate in Hz, read from the firmware now.
.It Va dev.rpi_fw_cpufreq.0.fw_min
.It Va dev.rpi_fw_cpufreq.0.fw_max
The lowest and the highest rate the firmware allows, in Hz.
.It Va dev.rpi_fw_cpufreq.0.core_voltage
The core voltage as the firmware reports it now, in the firmware's own
units.
.It Va dev.rpi_fw_cpufreq.0.sets
The number of changes of the clock the driver has asked the firmware for.
.It Va dev.rpi_fw_cpufreq.0.set_failures
The number of those the firmware refused or did not make.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "rpi_fw_cpufreq%d: CPU clock %u MHz, %u to %u MHz in %d levels"
The clock was found; printed once.
.It "rpi_fw_cpufreq%d: the firmware's range for the CPU clock, %u to %u Hz, is not usable"
The firmware reported no minimum, or a maximum below it.
There are no levels.
.It "rpi_fw_cpufreq%d: hw.rpi_fw_cpufreq.attach_freq: %u MHz asked for, error %d"
The result of the request the tunable made.
.It "rpi_fw_cpufreq%d: %u MHz asked for, the firmware reports %u MHz, error %d"
A change failed, or the rate read back afterwards was not the one asked for.
Printed at most once in ten seconds.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cpufreq 4 ,
.Xr rpi_fw_clk 4 ,
.Xr powerd 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The clock framework does not pass on a request for the rate it last set.
After the firmware has lowered the clock by itself, asking for the level
that was in force before is such a request: it fails, and is counted in
.Va set_failures .
Asking for a different level first works.
